The main idea is:

Gerund = Verb acting as a NOUN
Participle = Verb acting as an ADJECTIVE

1. Gerund

A verb + -ing acts like a noun.

Examples:

Swimming is good for health.
→ Swimming is the subject → noun → Gerund
He enjoys swimming.
→ Swimming is the object of enjoys → noun → Gerund
He is fond of swimming.
→ Swimming is the object of the preposition of → noun → Gerund
No smoking.
→ Smoking refers to the activity → noun → Gerund
2. Participle

An -ing form acts as an adjective when it describes a noun.

The crying baby needs milk.

Here, crying describes the noun baby.

Therefore:

crying → adjective → Present Participle




“Is the -ing word doing the job of a noun or describing a noun?”

                                            -ing word	                          Function	                              Answer
                                            Swimming is healthy.	              Subject/noun	                          Gerund
                                            I enjoy swimming.	                  Object/noun	                            Gerund
                                            The swimming boy is my brother.	    Describes boy	                          Participle
                                            The crying baby slept.	            Describes baby	                        Participle


for Gerund(use possesive like my,his,her,our,your)
for participle(use objective like me,us,him,you)

I don't mind his staying late hours. ✅ (not "him" bcoz staying is acting as gerund and noun so use posessive)
My dad appreciates my working hard. ✅ (not "me" same here)

Why "staying" and "working" count as nouns here

A word is functioning as a noun if it's doing a noun's job in the sentence — being the thing that's talked about, appreciated, minded, 
etc. — not describing an ongoing action of the subject.

Test it with the sentence structure

"I don't mind his staying late hours."

Ask: what don't I mind? → I don't mind "his staying late hours."

"My dad appreciates my working hard."

Ask: what does my dad appreciate? → He appreciates "my working hard."



✅ I insisted on his going there.

This is correct.

Structure:

insist on + noun / gerund

Here:

on = preposition
his = possessive form
going = gerund
there = adverb

So:

his going there = gerund phrase

Because on is a preposition, we cannot normally put an infinitive (to go) after it.


❌ I insisted him to go there.

This is incorrect in standard English.

Why?

Insist does NOT take:

❌ insist + person + to + V1

Instead, use:

insist on + noun/gerund

So:

❌ I insisted him to go there.
✅ I insisted on his going there.

Another correct structure

You can also say:

✅ I insisted that he go there.

Here the structure is:

insist + that + subject + base verb

So remember for exams:

INSIST ON + V-ing
INSIST THAT + subject + V1

Examples:

I insisted on his coming with us. ✅
I insisted that he come with us. ✅
I insisted him to come with us. ❌
